<SEOArticle
  title="Shoulder Mobility Routine for Lifters"
  description="Use this shoulder mobility routine before lifting to prepare the upper back, shoulder blades and shoulder rotation for pressing and pulling workouts."
  canonical="/recovery/shoulder-mobility-routine-for-lifters/"
  eyebrow="Mobility"
  heading="A shoulder mobility routine built for lifting days."
  lead="Good shoulder preparation is less about forcing a deep stretch and more about getting the upper back, shoulder blades and shoulder joint ready for the ranges you plan to use."
  {faqs}
  {toc}
  secondaryLabel="Recovery Guide"
  secondaryHref="/recovery/"
>
  <p><strong>Your warmup should prepare you to train, not tire you out.</strong> A few controlled movements can help you explore shoulder motion, organize the shoulder blades and gradually transition into pressing or pulling.</p>

  <h2 id="routine">8-minute shoulder mobility routine</h2>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card"><span class="pill">1 minute</span><h3>Upper-back rotations</h3><p>Use open-book rotations or quadruped thoracic rotations to move through the upper back without forcing the shoulder.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill coral">1 minute</span><h3>Wall slides</h3><p>Slide the arms upward while keeping the movement comfortable and allowing the shoulder blades to rotate naturally.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill orange">2 minutes</span><h3>Controlled shoulder circles</h3><p>Use slow arm circles or controlled rotations through the range you can own without shrugging or twisting excessively.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill purple">2 minutes</span><h3>Band pull-aparts or light rows</h3><p>Use very light resistance to practice scapular movement and upper-back control.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill">1 minute</span><h3>Incline push-up plus</h3><p>Use a wall or bench and let the shoulder blades move around the rib cage at the top of each repetition.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill coral">1 minute</span><h3>Ramp-up set</h3><p>Finish with the first exercise of the workout using a very light load before adding weight.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="pressing">Shoulder mobility before pressing</h2>
  <p>For bench press, overhead press or push-ups, prioritize the ranges you need. Upper-back extension and rotation, comfortable shoulder external rotation and natural shoulder-blade motion all contribute to pressing mechanics.</p>

  <p>A useful progression before bench press could be:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Upper-back rotation</li>
    <li>Wall slides</li>
    <li>Incline push-up plus</li>
    <li>Empty-bar or light dumbbell press</li>
    <li>Gradually heavier warmup sets</li>
  </ol>

  <p>For overhead pressing, spend extra time exploring overhead range without aggressively arching the lower back to create the appearance of more shoulder motion.</p>

  <h2 id="pulling">Shoulder mobility before pulling workouts</h2>
  <p>Rows, pull-downs and pull-ups also benefit from shoulder preparation. Light rows, hanging only if comfortable and controlled shoulder-blade motion can help you feel the difference between moving the arm and simply shrugging the shoulder.</p>

  <p>If pull-ups irritate the shoulder, do not assume more stretching is the answer. Adjust grip, range, load or exercise selection and use a pain-free pulling variation while you investigate the problem.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Warm up toward the workout.</strong> The most specific warmup is usually a lighter version of the exercise you are about to do.</div>

  <h2 id="strength">Mobility works better with strength</h2>
  <p>Active control helps make new range useful. Exercises such as rows, carries, controlled pressing, face pulls and light external-rotation work can build strength around the shoulder while the main lifts provide the larger training stimulus.</p>

  <p>Do not turn accessory work into an endless corrective routine. Choose a few movements that improve readiness or address a clear need, then return to the strength work that matters.</p>

  <h2>When to skip the routine</h2>
  <p>If a movement creates sharp pain, instability, numbness, weakness or symptoms that worsen with each session, stop using it. General mobility content cannot diagnose a shoulder problem.</p>

  <p>For a broader recovery plan between training sessions, see <a href="/recovery/mobility-recovery-after-workouts/">mobility and recovery after workouts</a>.</p>
</SEOArticle>
